RELEASE NOTES

In the year 2004, a rock and roll band was born in Columbus, Ohio, a big
small-town ripe with overlooked music talent. Two singer-songwriters who had met
in college at Miami University carefully hand-picked the best of the best in
Columbus’ music scene to create the band Embassy.

Embassy quickly got the attention of the music industry with their "Loft
Recordings" demo in 2005, which earned them a Top 10 ranking by MTV for the
“Best Unsigned Bands in the Nation”. After touring heavily to establish
themselves in markets like Cincinnati, Detroit, Chicago, D.C., and Philadelphia,
the band began to garner some media attention as well. Whether it was being
featured as a “Buzz Act” by HITS Magazine or being chosen as one of the “Top Ten
Unsigned Bands” by AlternativeAddiction.com – Embassy continued to make
headlines.


The beginning of 2006 brought about a much-anticipated publishing deal with
Sony/ATV. The deal, struck with Jon Nelson (who had also recently signed
FallOutBoy, We Are Scientists, Cartel, and Rise Against), gave Embassy the
resources needed to begin recording their first full-length album THE WAR OF
ART. The debut CD was met with instant media praise as Columbus Dispatch
journalist Aaron Beck declares, "The War of Art encorporates vocals that soar
atop thick, textured riffs; melodies galore; and heavy earnest lyrics. In short,
it's memorable, well-crafted modern rock with an exclamation point."

Some of the band’s most recent successes include placement on the new
Playstation video game MLB ’07 (May ‘07) as well as a featured spot on MTV’s
forthcoming documentary, “Bands on the Rise” (Feb. ‘07).

